                              Hands-on workshops are different because they force you to build. Here is why
                              doing beats listening every single time. 🧵
                              
                              2/5: Passive listening is a trap. It feels like you're learning because the
                              speaker is clear, but your brain isn't forming new connections.
                              
                              When you start "doing," you encounter friction. That friction is exactly where
                              the actual learning happens.
                              
                              3/5: Workshops give you a safe place to fail fast. In a lecture, you don't
                              realize you're confused until you're alone at your desk.
                              
                              In a workshop, you hit a wall, ask a question, and fix it in real-time. That
                              immediate feedback loop is a superpower.
                              
                              4/5: You build muscle memory, not just mental notes. Writing code, sketching a
                              design, or facilitating a session creates a physical anchor for the theory.
                              
                              You aren't just memorizing a list; you're developing a reflex.
                              
                              5/5: Knowledge is only useful if you can apply it. Next time you want to learn a
